window.docContents[103].resolve({"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-rigidity-every-copy-is-canonical":{"contents":"Every copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is of the form subcubeCopy b d. Note that a copy is\nan injective homomorphism: it preserves adjacency in the forward direction only,\nso, unlike for automorphisms, no distance or weight preservation is available. We use\nthe parallelogram lemma instead (hypercubeHamming_parallelogram). The proof has two\nsteps. First we read off the direction embedding d from the images of the basis\nvectors. Then we show that f x = f 0 + extendEmb d x by strong induction on the\nHamming weight of x. Vertices of weight 0 and 1 are 0 and the basis vectors,\nwhich are covered by the first step. A vertex y of weight at least 2 has two\ntrue coordinates i ≠ j. So it is the top corner of a 4-cycle whose other three\nvertices, y + eᵢ, y + eⱼ and y + eᵢ + eⱼ, have smaller weight, and the\nparallelogram lemma determines f y from their images.\n\nFirst we prove a small helper lemma: a vector of Hamming weight at least two has two\ndistinct true coordinates.\n\nprivate lemma exists_ne_true_true_of_one_lt_hammingNorm {n : ℕ} {y : Fin n → Bool}\n    (hy : 1 < hammingNorm y) : ∃ i j, i ≠ j ∧ y i = true ∧ y j = true := by\n  obtain ⟨i, hi, j, hj, hij⟩ := Finset.one_lt_card.mp hy\n  use i, j, hij\n  simp_all [Bool.zero_eq_false]\n\n\nA copy maps each square y, y + eᵢ, y + eᵢ + eⱼ, y + eⱼ of Q[k] to a 4-cycle\nin Q[n], so by the parallelogram lemma the image of any corner is the sum of the\nimages of the other three.\n\nprivate lemma copy_apply_eq_add_add {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) (y : Fin k → Bool)\n    {i j : Fin k} (hij : i ≠ j) :\n    f y = f (y + e i + e j) + f (y + e i) + f (y + e j) := by\n  have hadj : ∀ (x : Fin k → Bool) (a : Fin k),\n      (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f x) (f (x + e a)) :=\n    fun x a => f.toHom.map_adj (hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.2 ⟨a, rfl⟩)\n  -- three sides of the square y, y + e i, y + e i + e j, y + e j are mapped to\n  -- edges by hadj; for the fourth we reorder the sum\n  have h₄ : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f (y + e j)) (f (y + e i + e j)) := by\n    rw [add_right_comm]\n    exact hadj _ i\n  -- opposite corners of the square are distinct, and so are their images\n  have hne₁ : f y ≠ f (y + e i + e j) := fun h => by\n    simpa [Pi.single_apply, hij] using congrFun (f.injective h) i\n  have hne₂ : f (y + e i) ≠ f (y + e j) := fun h =>\n    hij (Pi.single_left_injective (by decide) (add_left_cancel (f.injective h)))\n  have hpar : f (y + e i) + f (y + e j) = f y + f (y + e i + e j) :=\n    hypercubeHamming_parallelogram (hadj y i) (hadj _ j) (hadj y j) h₄ hne₁ hne₂\n  -- substitute hpar and cancel the two copies of f (y + e i + e j)\n  rw [add_assoc, hpar, add_comm (f y), boolVec_add_add_cancel]\n\n\nFor the first step, each basis vector eᵢ is a neighbour of 0, so its image is a\nneighbour of f 0 and differs from it in a single coordinate, which we call d i.\nTwo basis vectors with the same direction would have the same image, so d is\ninjective because f is.\n\nprivate lemma exists_apply_single_eq_add_single {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) :\n    ∃ d : Fin k ↪ Fin n, ∀ i, f (e i) = f 0 + e (d i) := by\n  -- each basis vector is mapped to a neighbour of f 0, defining the\n  -- direction map d\n  have hD : ∀ i : Fin k, ∃ p : Fin n,\n      f (e i) = f 0 + e p := by\n    intro i\n    have hadj : (hypercubeHamming n).Adj (f 0) (f (e i)) :=\n      f.toHom.map_adj (hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.2 ⟨i, (zero_add _).symm⟩)\n    exact hypercubeHamming_adj_iff_exists.1 hadj\n  choose d hd using hD\n  have hdinj : Function.Injective d := by\n    intro i j hdij\n    have h : f (e i) = f (e j) := by\n      rw [hd i, hd j, hdij]\n    exact Pi.single_left_injective (by decide) (f.injective h)\n  exact ⟨⟨d, hdinj⟩, hd⟩\n\n\nFor the second step we show that the images of 0 and of the basis vectors\ndetermine the whole copy. Here the direction embedding d is an input rather than\nan output, so the statement applies to any d satisfying hd.\n\nprivate lemma copy_apply_eq_add_extendEmb_of_single {n k : ℕ}\n    {f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)} {d : Fin k ↪ Fin n}\n    (hd : ∀ i, f (e i) = f 0 + e (d i))\n    (x : Fin k → Bool) : f x = f 0 + extendEmb d x := by\n  suffices H : ∀ (m : ℕ) (y : Fin k → Bool), hammingNorm y = m →\n      f y = f 0 + extendEmb d y from H _ x rfl\n  intro m\n  induction m using Nat.strong_induction_on with\n  | _ m ih =>\n    rcases m with _ | m\n    · -- weight zero: y = 0\n      intro y hy\n      rw [hammingNorm_eq_zero] at hy\n      simp [hy]\n    rcases m with _ | m\n    · -- weight one: y is a basis vector, handled by hd\n      intro y hy\n      obtain ⟨i, rfl⟩ := hammingNorm_eq_one_iff.mp hy\n      rw [hd i, extendEmb_single]\n    -- weight m + 2: y has two distinct true coordinates i ≠ j\n    intro y hy\n    obtain ⟨i, j, hij, hyi, hyj⟩ :=\n      exists_ne_true_true_of_one_lt_hammingNorm (by grind : 1 < hammingNorm y)\n    -- the three vertices below y in the four-cycle have smaller weight\n    have hn₁ : hammingNorm (y + e i) = m + 1 := by\n      grind [hammingNorm_add_single hyi]\n    have hn₂ : hammingNorm (y + e j) = m + 1 := by\n      grind [hammingNorm_add_single hyj]\n    have h2j : (y + e i : Fin k → Bool) j = true := by\n      simp [hij.symm, hyj]\n    have hn₃ : hammingNorm (y + e i + e j) = m := by\n      grind [hammingNorm_add_single h2j]\n    have hf₁ := ih (m + 1) (by grind) _ hn₁\n    have hf₂ := ih (m + 1) (by grind) _ hn₂\n    have hf₃ := ih m (by grind) _ hn₃\n    -- f y is determined by the three smaller corners of the square\n    rw [copy_apply_eq_add_add f y hij, hf₁, hf₂, hf₃]\n    simp [extendEmb_add, add_assoc, add_comm, add_left_comm]\n\n\nCombining the two steps:\n\nprivate lemma exists_apply_eq_add_extendEmb {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) :\n    ∃ d : Fin k ↪ Fin n, ∀ x, f x = f 0 + extendEmb d x := by\n  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := exists_apply_single_eq_add_single f\n  exact ⟨d, copy_apply_eq_add_extendEmb_of_single hd⟩\n\n\nEvery copy of Q[k] in Q[n] is a canonical subcube copy.\n\nlemma exists_eq_subcubeCopy {n k : ℕ}\n    (f : Copy (hypercubeHamming k) (hypercubeHamming n)) :\n    ∃ (b : Fin n → Bool) (d : Fin k ↪ Fin n), f = subcubeCopy b d := by\n  obtain ⟨d, hd⟩ := exists_apply_eq_add_extendEmb f\n  exact ⟨f 0, d, Copy.ext fun x => by simpa using hd x⟩\n\nend hypercubeHamming\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Algebraic Graph Theory\u0009Subcubes of the Hypercube*","header":"12.8.3. Rigidity: Every Copy is Canonical","id":"/Algebraic-Graph-Theory/#section-rigidity-every-copy-is-canonical"},"/Cliques/#section-exercises-cliques":{"contents":"1. Show that any finite subset t of a clique s is a #t-clique.\n\nend SimpleGraph\nend\nnamespace SimpleGraph\n\n\nopen Finset\nvariable {V : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V}\n\nexample {s : Set V} {t : Finset V} (hc : G.IsClique s) (h : ↑t ⊆ s) :\n    G.IsNClique #t t := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that {0, 1, 2} is a triangle in the Paley graph on 17 vertices.\n\nexample : (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).IsNClique 3 {0, 1, 2} := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices is 4-clique-free. Hint: decide will\nprobably time out, so use native_decide instead.\n\nlemma cliqueFree_paleyGraph_seventeen_four' :\n    (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).CliqueFree 4 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Show that the clique number of finite graphs is monotone: if G ≤ H then\nω G ≤ ω H. (There is a one-line proof using a lemma from the chapter, and a\ndirect proof using exists_isNClique_cliqueNum.)\n\nlemma cliqueNum_mono' [Finite V] {H : SimpleGraph V} (h : G ≤ H) : ω G ≤ ω H := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Show that the clique number of the Paley graph on 17 vertices is 3. (Recall\nthat we have IsNClique.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree, so we just need to give an\nexample of a 3-clique and show that the graph is 4-clique-free.)\n\nexample : ω (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)) = 3 := by\n  sorry\n\n\nIn the next few exercises we look at the clique number of graph products starting\nwith the tensor product G ⊗g H. Recall that two vertices (v₁, w₁), (v₂, w₂) of\nthe tensor product are adjacent if and only if G.Adj v₁ v₂ and H.Adj w₁ w₂.\n\n6. The first projection of a clique in the tensor product G ⊗g H is a clique in\nG.\n\nexample {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set (V × W)}\n    (h : (G ⊗g H).IsClique s) : G.IsClique (Prod.fst '' s) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. If G is n-clique-free then so is its tensor product G ⊗g H with any graph\nH.\n\nlemma CliqueFree.tensorProd_left' {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {n : ℕ}\n    (h : G.CliqueFree n) : (G ⊗g H).CliqueFree n := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. The j-th projection of a clique in the indexed tensor product ⨂g i, G i is a\nclique in G j.\n\nexample {ι : Type*} {U : ι → Type*} {G : (i : ι) → SimpleGraph (U i)} (j : ι)\n    {s : Set ((i : ι) → U i)} (h : (⨂g i, G i).IsClique s) :\n    (G j).IsClique ((· j) '' s) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. The clique number of G ⊗g H is min (ω G) (ω H) (for finite graphs). Hint:\nfor the lower bound, let m = min (ω G) (ω H) and use\nnot_cliqueFree_iff_top_isContained to find copies of K[m] in both G and H.\nPair them with tensorProd.prod to get a homomorphism K[m] →g G ⊗g H.\n\nlemma cliqueNum_tensorProd' {W : Type*} [Finite V] [Finite W] {H : SimpleGraph W} :\n    ω (G ⊗g H) = min (ω G) (ω H) := by\n  sorry\n\n\nWe now turn to the box product G □ H. Recall that two vertices (v₁, w₁), (v₂, w₂)\nof the box product are adjacent if and only if either G.Adj v₁ v₂ and w₁ = w₂, or\nv₁ = v₂ and H.Adj w₁ w₂. This implies that cliques are always constant in one\ncoordinate.\n\n10. A clique s in G gives a \"row\" clique in the box product G □ H (so long as\nthere is a vertex w₀ in H to fix the second coordinate).\n\nexample {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set V} (w₀ : W) (h : G.IsClique s) :\n    (G □ H).IsClique ((·, w₀) '' s) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n11. In any finite box product G □ H with nonempty second factor we have\nω G ≤ ω (G □ H): the embedding boxProdLeft can transport a maximum clique of G\nto a clique of the same size in G □ H.\n\nlemma cliqueNum_le_cliqueNum_boxProd_left' {W : Type*} [Finite V] [Finite W]\n    [Nonempty W] {H : SimpleGraph W} : ω G ≤ ω (G □ H) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n12. A clique of G □ H containing a \"horizontal\" edge (two vertices sharing their\nsecond coordinate) is constant in the second coordinate.\n\nlemma IsClique.boxProd_snd_eq' {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set (V × W)}\n    {x y : V × W} (hs : (G □ H).IsClique s) (hx : x ∈ s) (hy : y ∈ s)\n    (hG : G.Adj x.1 y.1) (hsnd : x.2 = y.2) : ∀ z ∈ s, z.2 = x.2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n13. Prod.fst is injective on a clique of G □ H whose vertices all share the same\nsecond coordinate, so such a clique projects to a clique of G of the same size.\n\nlemma IsNClique.boxProd_image_fst' {W : Type*} [DecidableEq V] {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    {m : ℕ} {t : Finset (V × W)} {w : W} (ht : (G □ H).IsNClique m t)\n    (hconst : ∀ z ∈ t, z.2 = w) : G.IsNClique m (t.image Prod.fst) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n14. If G is finite then a clique of G □ H containing a \"horizontal\" edge has\nsize at most ω G.\n\nlemma IsNClique.boxProd_le_cliqueNum_left' {W : Type*} [Finite V]\n    {H : SimpleGraph W} {m : ℕ} {t : Finset (V × W)} {x y : V × W}\n    (ht : (G □ H).IsNClique m t) (hx : x ∈ t) (hy : y ∈ t) (hG : G.Adj x.1 y.1)\n    (hsnd : x.2 = y.2) : m ≤ ω G := by\n  sorry\n\n\n15. The clique number of G □ H is max (ω G) (ω H) (for finite nonempty graphs).\n\nlemma cliqueNum_boxProd' {W : Type*} [Finite V] [Finite W] [Nonempty V]\n    [Nonempty W] {H : SimpleGraph W} :\n    ω (G □ H) = max (ω G) (ω H) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n16. The clique number of the hypercube Q[n] is 1 for n = 0 and 2 otherwise.\n(There are easier ways to prove this: for instance, once we have colourings we could\nuse a two-colouring to prove ω Q[n] ≤ 2, but we can also use the result of the\nprevious exercise.)\n\nlemma cliqueNum_hypercube' {n : ℕ} : ω Q[n] = if n = 0 then 1 else 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n17. Show that a set of vertices is a clique in G if and only if it is an\nindependent set in the complement of G. (Mathlib has this as isIndepSet_compl,\nbut prove it directly from the definitions, using compl_adj.)\n\nexample {s : Set V} : G.IsClique s ↔ Gᶜ.IsIndepSet s := by\n  sorry\n\n\n18. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices has independence number 3. Together\nwith Exercise 3 this shows that there is a graph on 17 vertices with no 4-clique\nand no independent set of size 4, so the Ramsey number R(4, 4) is at least 18.\n(In fact R(4, 4) = 18.) Hint: as in the chapter, rewrite with cliqueFree_compl,\nthen use native_decide as in Exercise 3.\n\nexample : ⍺ (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)) = 3 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n19. Show that a clique and an independent set share at most one vertex, and deduce\nthat ω G + ⍺ G ≤ Nat.card V + 1 in any finite graph.\n\nlemma IsClique.card_inter_le_one_of_isIndepSet' [DecidableEq V] {s t : Finset V}\n    (hs : G.IsClique ↑s) (ht : G.IsIndepSet ↑t) : #(s ∩ t) ≤ 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\nlemma cliqueNum_add_indepNum_le' [Finite V] : ω G + ⍺ G ≤ Nat.card V + 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n20. Show that any graph on at least 6 vertices contains a triangle or an\nindependent set of size 3, so that R(3, 3) = 6. Hint: suppose not, and apply\ndegree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree to both G and Gᶜ, bounding both\nindependence numbers with CliqueFree.cliqueNum_le. Mathlib's degree_compl\nrelates the degrees of a vertex in G and Gᶜ.\n\ntheorem not_cliqueFree_three_and_indepSetFree_three' [Fintype V]\n    (h : 6 ≤ Fintype.card V) : ¬(G.CliqueFree 3 ∧ G.IndepSetFree 3) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n21. Unlike the junk value ⍺ pathNat = 0 of the independence number, the clique\nnumber of the infinite path on ℕ is what you expect. Show that ω pathNat = 2.\nHint: pathNat is triangle-free, since adjacent vertices have different parity. The\nlemma IsNClique.le_cliqueNum needs [Finite V], but\nIsNClique.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree needs no finiteness.\n\nexample : ω pathNat = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\n22. Show that the Kneser graph Kn[n, 1] is isomorphic to the complete graph K[n].\n(Its vertices are the singletons {a}, and distinct singletons are disjoint.)\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : Nonempty (Kn[n, 1] ≃g K[n]) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n23. Compute the clique number of the Mycielskian of the edgeless graph ⊥. The\nanswer depends on whether V is empty. If V is empty then mμ(⊥) has only the\nvertex none, so its clique number is 1. If V is nonempty then none and vʳ\nare adjacent and mμ(⊥) is triangle-free, so its clique number is 2.\n\nlemma cliqueNum_mycielskian_bot_of_isEmpty' [IsEmpty V] :\n    ω mμ((⊥ : SimpleGraph V)) = 1 := by\n  sorry\n\n\nlemma cliqueNum_mycielskian_bot' [Nonempty V] :\n    ω mμ((⊥ : SimpleGraph V)) = 2 := by\n  sorry\n\n\nThe last few exercises are on independent sets in complete bipartite graphs (and\ntheir Mycielskians).\n\n24. In a complete bipartite graph the non-neighbours of a left vertex are precisely\nthe left part, and similarly for right vertices.\n\nlemma completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inl_iff' {W : Type*} {v : V} {x : V ⊕ W} :\n    ¬(completeBipartiteGraph V W).Adj (.inl v) x ↔\n      x ∈ Set.range .inl := by\n  sorry\n\n\nlemma completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inr_iff' {W : Type*} {w : W} {x : V ⊕ W} :\n    ¬(completeBipartiteGraph V W).Adj (.inr w) x ↔\n      x ∈ Set.range .inr := by\n  sorry\n\n\n25. An independent set of a complete bipartite graph is contained entirely in the\nleft part or entirely in the right part: if it contains a left vertex then all its\nvertices are non-neighbours of that vertex, so all lie in the left part.\n\nlemma completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_subset_left_or_right' {W : Type*}\n    {s : Set (V ⊕ W)} (hs : (completeBipartiteGraph V W).IsIndepSet s) :\n    s ⊆ Set.range .inl ∨ s ⊆ Set.range .inr := by\n  sorry\n\n\n26. Any Finset contained in the range of a function f : α → β with finite\ndomain has at most Nat.card α elements. In particular a Finset (V ⊕ W) contained\nin the left part has at most Nat.card V elements (and similarly on the right),\nsince the left part is the range of Sum.inl.\n\nlemma _root_.Finset.card_le_of_subset_range' {α β : Type*} [Finite α] {f : α → β}\n    {s : Finset β} (h : ↑s ⊆ Set.range f) : #s ≤ Nat.card α := by\n  sorry\n\n\n27. Any set of vertices contained in one part of a complete bipartite graph is\nindependent, since a part contains no edges (we only need this for the right part).\n\nlemma isIndepSet_completeBipartiteGraph_of_subset_range_inr' {W : Type*}\n    {s : Set (V ⊕ W)} (h : s ⊆ Set.range .inr) :\n    (completeBipartiteGraph V W).IsIndepSet s := by\n  sorry\n\n\n28. The independence number of a complete bipartite graph is the size of the larger\npart; we state this as ⍺ K[a, b] = b when a ≤ b. The right part is a b-element\nindependent set, and by Exercises 25 and 26 no independent set is larger.\n\nlemma indepNum_completeBipartiteGraph' {a b : ℕ} (h : a ≤ b) :\n    ⍺ K[a, b] = b := by\n  sorry\n\n\nIf we want to bound the independence number of the Mycielskian of a complete\nbipartite graph we need a bound on the size of any independent set s of K[a, b]\nplus the size of the complement of its open neighbourhood N(s)ᶜ. We give this bound\nin the next exercise. The last exercise uses le_indepNum_mycielskian and\nmycielskian_indepSet_card_le from the starred section\nIndependence Number of the Mycielskian.\n\n29. Any independent set s of K[a, b] (with a ≤ b) satisfies\n#s + #N(s)ᶜ ≤ 2 * b. We state this with an abstract finset t in place of\nN(s)ᶜ, characterized by its membership property, so that the lemma applies\nwhatever decidability instances N(s)ᶜ is written with. If s is empty then t\nis all a + b vertices; otherwise s lies in a single part (Exercise 25) and\nt lies in the same part (every vertex of the other part is a neighbour of any\nvertex of s), so each has size at most that of the part.\n\nlemma completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_card_add_le' {a b : ℕ} (hab : a ≤ b)\n    {s t : Finset (Fin a ⊕ Fin b)} (hs : K[a, b].IsIndepSet ↑s)\n    (ht : ∀ x, x ∈ t ↔ ∀ y ∈ s, ¬K[a, b].Adj y x) :\n    #s + #t ≤ 2 * b := by\n  sorry\n\n\n30. The independence number of the Mycielskian of K[a, b] is 2 * b whenever\na ≤ b and b ≠ 0: the right part B is an independent set with B ⊆ N(B)ᶜ,\nso it contributes at least 2 * b, and no independent set does better by\nExercise 29. (Note that b ≠ 0 is needed: mμ(K[0, 0]) still has the vertex\nnone, so its independence number is 1.)\n\nlemma indepNum_mycielskian_completeBipartiteGraph' {a b : ℕ} [NeZero b]\n    (h : a ≤ b) : ⍺ mμ(K[a, b]) = 2 * b := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that any finite subset t of a clique s is a #t-clique.example {s : Set V} {t : Finset V} (hc : G.IsClique s) (h : ↑t ⊆ s) :\n    G.IsNClique #t t :=\n  ⟨hc.subset h, rfl⟩\n2. Show that {0, 1, 2} is a triangle in the Paley graph on 17 vertices.example : (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).IsNClique 3 {0, 1, 2} := by decide\n3. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices is 4-clique-free. Hint: decide will\nprobably time out, so use native_decide instead.lemma cliqueFree_paleyGraph_seventeen_four :\n    (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).CliqueFree 4 := by\n  native_decide\n4. Show that the clique number of finite graphs is monotone: if G ≤ H then\nω G ≤ ω H. (There is a one-line proof using a lemma from the chapter, and a\ndirect proof using exists_isNClique_cliqueNum.)lemma cliqueNum_mono [Finite V] {H : SimpleGraph V} (h : G ≤ H) : ω G ≤ ω H :=\n  -- G ≤ H gives a homomorphism Hom.ofLE h : G →g H, so this is a special case of\n  -- Hom.cliqueNum_le. Alternatively, directly: a clique s in G achieving the\n  -- clique number of G is also a clique in H, so its size is at most ω H:\n  -- obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := G.exists_isNClique_cliqueNum; exact (hs.mono h).le_cliqueNum\n  (Hom.ofLE h).cliqueNum_le\n5. Show that the clique number of the Paley graph on 17 vertices is 3. (Recall\nthat we have IsNClique.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree, so we just need to give an\nexample of a 3-clique and show that the graph is 4-clique-free.)example : ω (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)) = 3 := by\n  -- the Paley graph on 17 vertices is 4-clique-free (Exercise 3)\n  have h4 : (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).CliqueFree 4 :=\n    cliqueFree_paleyGraph_seventeen_four\n  -- and it contains a triangle (Exercise 2)\n  have h3 : (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).IsNClique 3 {0, 1, 2} := by decide\n  exact h3.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree h4\nIn the next few exercises we look at the clique number of graph products starting\nwith the tensor product G ⊗g H. Recall that two vertices (v₁, w₁), (v₂, w₂) of\nthe tensor product are adjacent if and only if G.Adj v₁ v₂ and H.Adj w₁ w₂.6. The first projection of a clique in the tensor product G ⊗g H is a clique in\nG.example {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set (V × W)}\n    (h : (G ⊗g H).IsClique s) : G.IsClique (Prod.fst '' s) := by\n  -- take two distinct vertices in the image, lifted to (v₁, w₁), (v₂, w₂) ∈ s\n  rintro _ ⟨⟨v₁, w₁⟩, hp, rfl⟩ _ ⟨⟨v₂, w₂⟩, hq, rfl⟩ hne\n  -- the lifts are distinct, so adjacent; adjacency in G ⊗g H gives G.Adj v₁ v₂\n  exact (h hp hq (fun he => hne (congrArg Prod.fst he))).1\n7. If G is n-clique-free then so is its tensor product G ⊗g H with any graph\nH.lemma CliqueFree.tensorProd_left {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {n : ℕ}\n    (h : G.CliqueFree n) : (G ⊗g H).CliqueFree n :=\n  -- unlike the box product, the first projection is a homomorphism G ⊗g H →g G\n  -- (tensorProd.fstHom), so it maps any n-clique to an n-clique of G\n  h.of_hom tensorProd.fstHom\n8. The j-th projection of a clique in the indexed tensor product ⨂g i, G i is a\nclique in G j.example {ι : Type*} {U : ι → Type*} {G : (i : ι) → SimpleGraph (U i)} (j : ι)\n    {s : Set ((i : ι) → U i)} (h : (⨂g i, G i).IsClique s) :\n    (G j).IsClique ((· j) '' s) := by\n  rintro _ ⟨f, hf, rfl⟩ _ ⟨g, hg, rfl⟩ hne\n  -- f and g differ at j, so are distinct, hence adjacent in every coordinate\n  have hadj := h hf hg (fun he => hne (congrFun he j))\n  rw [piTensorProd_adj] at hadj\n  exact hadj.1 j\n9. The clique number of G ⊗g H is min (ω G) (ω H) (for finite graphs). Hint:\nfor the lower bound, let m = min (ω G) (ω H) and use\nnot_cliqueFree_iff_top_isContained to find copies of K[m] in both G and H.\nPair them with tensorProd.prod to get a homomorphism K[m] →g G ⊗g H.lemma cliqueNum_tensorProd {W : Type*} [Finite V] [Finite W] {H : SimpleGraph W} :\n    ω (G ⊗g H) = min (ω G) (ω H) := by\n  classical\n  apply le_antisymm\n  · -- upper bound: both projections tensorProd.fstHom, tensorProd.sndHom are\n    -- homomorphisms, so the clique number cannot exceed either factor's\n    exact le_min tensorProd.fstHom.cliqueNum_le tensorProd.sndHom.cliqueNum_le\n  · -- lower bound: with m := min (ω G) (ω H), neither G nor H is\n    -- m-clique-free (each has a clique of size at least m)\n    set m := min (ω G) (ω H)\n    obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := G.exists_isNClique_cliqueNum\n    obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := H.exists_isNClique_cliqueNum\n    have hG : ¬G.CliqueFree m := fun hf => hf.mono (min_le_left ..) s hs\n    have hH : ¬H.CliqueFree m := fun hf => hf.mono (min_le_right ..) t ht\n    -- so both contain a copy of the complete graph on Fin m\n    rw [not_cliqueFree_iff_top_isContained] at hG hH\n    obtain ⟨f⟩ := hG\n    obtain ⟨g⟩ := hH\n    -- pairing the two copies gives a homomorphism from ⊤ into G ⊗g H\n    simpa using (tensorProd.prod f.toHom g.toHom).cliqueNum_le\nWe now turn to the box product G □ H. Recall that two vertices (v₁, w₁), (v₂, w₂)\nof the box product are adjacent if and only if either G.Adj v₁ v₂ and w₁ = w₂, or\nv₁ = v₂ and H.Adj w₁ w₂. This implies that cliques are always constant in one\ncoordinate.10. A clique s in G gives a \"row\" clique in the box product G □ H (so long as\nthere is a vertex w₀ in H to fix the second coordinate).example {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set V} (w₀ : W) (h : G.IsClique s) :\n    (G □ H).IsClique ((·, w₀) '' s) := by\n  rintro _ ⟨a, ha, rfl⟩ _ ⟨b, hb, rfl⟩ hne\n  -- both vertices share the second coordinate w₀, so they are adjacent in G □ H\n  -- precisely because a and b are adjacent in G.\n  rw [boxProd_adj]\n  exact Or.inl ⟨h ha hb (fun he => hne (by rw [he])), rfl⟩\n11. In any finite box product G □ H with nonempty second factor we have\nω G ≤ ω (G □ H): the embedding boxProdLeft can transport a maximum clique of G\nto a clique of the same size in G □ H.lemma cliqueNum_le_cliqueNum_boxProd_left {W : Type*} [Finite V] [Finite W]\n    [Nonempty W] {H : SimpleGraph W} : ω G ≤ ω (G □ H) :=\n  (boxProdLeft G H (Classical.arbitrary W)).toHom.cliqueNum_le\n12. A clique of G □ H containing a \"horizontal\" edge (two vertices sharing their\nsecond coordinate) is constant in the second coordinate.lemma IsClique.boxProd_snd_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} {s : Set (V × W)}\n    {x y : V × W} (hs : (G □ H).IsClique s) (hx : x ∈ s) (hy : y ∈ s)\n    (hG : G.Adj x.1 y.1) (hsnd : x.2 = y.2) : ∀ z ∈ s, z.2 = x.2 := by\n  intro z hz\n  -- z is equal or adjacent to both x and y; if z differed from x in the\n  -- second coordinate it would share its first coordinate with both, forcing\n  -- x.1 = y.1, which contradicts G.Adj x.1 y.1\n  have h1 := hs hz hx\n  have h2 := hs hz hy\n  grind [boxProd_adj]\n13. Prod.fst is injective on a clique of G □ H whose vertices all share the same\nsecond coordinate, so such a clique projects to a clique of G of the same size.lemma IsNClique.boxProd_image_fst {W : Type*} [DecidableEq V] {H : SimpleGraph W}\n    {m : ℕ} {t : Finset (V × W)} {w : W} (ht : (G □ H).IsNClique m t)\n    (hconst : ∀ z ∈ t, z.2 = w) : G.IsNClique m (t.image Prod.fst) := by\n  -- it suffices to show that Prod.fst preserves adjacency on t (injectivity on\n  -- the clique t is then automatic); an edge of t cannot be vertical, since the\n  -- vertices of t agree in the second coordinate\n  refine ht.image_of_adj fun p hp q hq h => ?_\n  grind [boxProd_adj]\n14. If G is finite then a clique of G □ H containing a \"horizontal\" edge has\nsize at most ω G.lemma IsNClique.boxProd_le_cliqueNum_left {W : Type*} [Finite V]\n    {H : SimpleGraph W} {m : ℕ} {t : Finset (V × W)} {x y : V × W}\n    (ht : (G □ H).IsNClique m t) (hx : x ∈ t) (hy : y ∈ t) (hG : G.Adj x.1 y.1)\n    (hsnd : x.2 = y.2) : m ≤ ω G := by\n  classical\n  -- every vertex of t shares the second coordinate x.2\n  have hconst : ∀ z ∈ t, z.2 = x.2 :=\n    fun z hz => ht.isClique.boxProd_snd_eq hx hy hG hsnd z hz\n  exact (ht.boxProd_image_fst hconst).le_cliqueNum\n15. The clique number of G □ H is max (ω G) (ω H) (for finite nonempty graphs).lemma cliqueNum_boxProd {W : Type*} [Finite V] [Finite W] [Nonempty V]\n    [Nonempty W] {H : SimpleGraph W} :\n    ω (G □ H) = max (ω G) (ω H) := by\n  classical\n  apply le_antisymm\n  · -- upper bound: a maximum clique has an edge, horizontal or vertical\n    obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := (G □ H).exists_isNClique_cliqueNum\n    rcases Nat.lt_or_ge #t 2 with hsmall | hbig\n    · -- a clique of size at most one is bounded since ω G ≥ 1\n      grind [ht.card_eq, one_le_cliqueNum]\n    · -- pick two distinct adjacent vertices x, y\n      obtain ⟨x, hx, y, hy, hxy⟩ := Finset.one_lt_card.mp hbig\n      have hadj := ht.isClique hx hy hxy\n      rw [boxProd_adj] at hadj\n      rcases hadj with ⟨hG, hsnd⟩ | ⟨hH, hfst⟩\n      · -- horizontal edge: bound the clique directly by ω G\n        exact (ht.boxProd_le_cliqueNum_left hx hy hG hsnd).trans (le_max_left _ _)\n      · -- vertical edge: swap the factors with boxProdComm to turn it into a\n        -- horizontal edge of H □ G, then reuse the same bound, now giving ω H\n        have htH := ht.image (boxProdComm ..).toHom\n        exact (htH.boxProd_le_cliqueNum_left (Finset.mem_image_of_mem _ hx)\n          (Finset.mem_image_of_mem _ hy) hH hfst).trans (le_max_right _ _)\n  · -- lower bound: a row realizes ω G (boxProdLeft) and a column realizes ω H\n    -- (boxProdRight); both are graph embeddings into G □ H\n    exact max_le cliqueNum_le_cliqueNum_boxProd_left\n      (boxProdRight G H (Classical.arbitrary V)).toHom.cliqueNum_le\n16. The clique number of the hypercube Q[n] is 1 for n = 0 and 2 otherwise.\n(There are easier ways to prove this: for instance, once we have colourings we could\nuse a two-colouring to prove ω Q[n] ≤ 2, but we can also use the result of the\nprevious exercise.)lemma cliqueNum_hypercube {n : ℕ} : ω Q[n] = if n = 0 then 1 else 2 := by\n  induction n with\n  | zero =>\n    exact one_le_cliqueNum.antisymm' (by simp)\n  | succ n ih =>\n    simp only [Nat.add_eq_zero_iff, one_ne_zero, and_false, ↓reduceIte]\n    have := cliqueNum_boxProd (G := Q[n]) (H := (⊤ : SimpleGraph Bool))\n    rw [ih, cliqueNum_top] at this\n    cases n <;> simpa\n17. Show that a set of vertices is a clique in G if and only if it is an\nindependent set in the complement of G. (Mathlib has this as isIndepSet_compl,\nbut prove it directly from the definitions, using compl_adj.)example {s : Set V} : G.IsClique s ↔ Gᶜ.IsIndepSet s := by\n  constructor\n  · -- distinct vertices of a clique are adjacent in G, so not adjacent in Gᶜ\n    intro h x hx y hy hne hadj\n    exact ((compl_adj G x y).mp hadj).2 (h hx hy hne)\n  · -- if distinct x, y were not adjacent in G they would be adjacent in Gᶜ\n    intro h x hx y hy hne\n    by_contra hnadj\n    exact h hx hy hne ((compl_adj G x y).mpr ⟨hne, hnadj⟩)\n18. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices has independence number 3. Together\nwith Exercise 3 this shows that there is a graph on 17 vertices with no 4-clique\nand no independent set of size 4, so the Ramsey number R(4, 4) is at least 18.\n(In fact R(4, 4) = 18.) Hint: as in the chapter, rewrite with cliqueFree_compl,\nthen use native_decide as in Exercise 3.example : ⍺ (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)) = 3 := by\n  -- {0, 3, 6} is independent since 3 and 6 are not squares mod 17\n  have h3 : (paleyGraph (ZMod 17)).IsNIndepSet 3 {0, 3, 6} := by decide\n  exact h3.indepNum_eq_of_indepSetFree (by rw [← cliqueFree_compl]; native_decide)\n19. Show that a clique and an independent set share at most one vertex, and deduce\nthat ω G + ⍺ G ≤ Nat.card V + 1 in any finite graph.lemma IsClique.card_inter_le_one_of_isIndepSet [DecidableEq V] {s t : Finset V}\n    (hs : G.IsClique ↑s) (ht : G.IsIndepSet ↑t) : #(s ∩ t) ≤ 1 := by\n  -- two distinct common vertices would be both adjacent and non-adjacent\n  rw [Finset.card_le_one]\n  intro x hx y hy\n  by_contra hne\n  simp only [Finset.mem_inter] at hx hy\n  exact ht hx.2 hy.2 hne (hs hx.1 hy.1 hne)\nlemma cliqueNum_add_indepNum_le [Finite V] : ω G + ⍺ G ≤ Nat.card V + 1 := by\n  classical\n  have := Fintype.ofFinite V\n  obtain ⟨s, hs⟩ := G.exists_isNClique_cliqueNum\n  obtain ⟨t, ht⟩ := G.exists_isNIndepSet_indepNum\n  -- a maximum clique and a maximum independent set share at most one vertex\n  have hst : #(s ∩ t) ≤ 1 :=\n    hs.isClique.card_inter_le_one_of_isIndepSet ht.isIndepSet\n  -- inclusion-exclusion, and s ∪ t has at most Nat.card V elements\n  have := Finset.card_union_add_card_inter s t\n  have := (s ∪ t).card_le_univ\n  rw [← hs.card_eq, ← ht.card_eq, Nat.card_eq_fintype_card]\n  grind\n20. Show that any graph on at least 6 vertices contains a triangle or an\nindependent set of size 3, so that R(3, 3) = 6. Hint: suppose not, and apply\ndegree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree to both G and Gᶜ, bounding both\nindependence numbers with CliqueFree.cliqueNum_le. Mathlib's degree_compl\nrelates the degrees of a vertex in G and Gᶜ.theorem not_cliqueFree_three_and_indepSetFree_three [Fintype V]\n    (h : 6 ≤ Fintype.card V) : ¬(G.CliqueFree 3 ∧ G.IndepSetFree 3) := by\n  classical\n  rintro ⟨h3, hi3⟩\n  -- Gᶜ is also triangle-free, since G has no independent set of size 3\n  have hc3 : Gᶜ.CliqueFree 3 := (G.cliqueFree_compl).mpr hi3\n  obtain ⟨v⟩ : Nonempty V := Fintype.card_pos_iff.mp (by grind)\n  -- so v has degree at most ⍺ G in G and at most ⍺ Gᶜ in Gᶜ\n  have h1 := degree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree h3 v\n  have h2 := degree_le_indepNum_of_triangleFree hc3 v\n  -- and both independence numbers are at most 2\n  have hi : ⍺ G ≤ 2 := by simpa using hc3.cliqueNum_le\n  have hic : ⍺ Gᶜ ≤ 2 := by simpa using h3.cliqueNum_le\n  -- but the two degrees add up to Fintype.card V - 1 ≥ 5\n  have := G.degree_compl v\n  grind\n21. Unlike the junk value ⍺ pathNat = 0 of the independence number, the clique\nnumber of the infinite path on ℕ is what you expect. Show that ω pathNat = 2.\nHint: pathNat is triangle-free, since adjacent vertices have different parity. The\nlemma IsNClique.le_cliqueNum needs [Finite V], but\nIsNClique.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree needs no finiteness.example : ω pathNat = 2 := by\n  -- pathNat is triangle-free: adjacent vertices have different parity\n  have h3 : pathNat.CliqueFree 3 := by\n    intro t ht\n    obtain ⟨a, b, c, hab, hac, hbc, rfl⟩ := card_eq_three.mp ht.card_eq\n    have h1 : pathNat.Adj a b := ht.isClique (by grind) (by grind) hab\n    have h2 : pathNat.Adj a c := ht.isClique (by grind) (by grind) hac\n    have h3 : pathNat.Adj b c := ht.isClique (by grind) (by grind) hbc\n    grind\n  -- the edge {0, 1} is a 2-clique, and IsNClique.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree\n  -- needs no finiteness: h3 bounds the size of every clique\n  have h2 : pathNat.IsNClique 2 {0, 1} := is2Clique_pair_iff.mpr (by decide)\n  exact h2.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree h3\n22. Show that the Kneser graph Kn[n, 1] is isomorphic to the complete graph K[n].\n(Its vertices are the singletons {a}, and distinct singletons are disjoint.)example (n : ℕ) : Nonempty (Kn[n, 1] ≃g K[n]) := by\n  -- the map a ↦ {a} is a bijection from Fin n to the vertices of Kn[n, 1]\n  let f : Fin n → {s : Finset (Fin n) // #s = 1} := fun a => ⟨{a}, card_singleton a⟩\n  have hf : Function.Bijective f := by\n    refine ⟨fun a b h => by simpa [f] using congrArg Subtype.val h, fun s => ?_⟩\n    obtain ⟨a, ha⟩ := card_eq_one.mp s.2\n    exact ⟨a, Subtype.ext ha.symm⟩\n  refine ⟨⟨(Equiv.ofBijective f hf).symm, ?_⟩⟩\n  -- it remains to check that adjacency is preserved\n  intro a b\n  obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hf.2 a\n  obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := hf.2 b\n  simp [f]\n23. Compute the clique number of the Mycielskian of the edgeless graph ⊥. The\nanswer depends on whether V is empty. If V is empty then mμ(⊥) has only the\nvertex none, so its clique number is 1. If V is nonempty then none and vʳ\nare adjacent and mμ(⊥) is triangle-free, so its clique number is 2.lemma cliqueNum_mycielskian_bot_of_isEmpty [IsEmpty V] :\n    ω mμ((⊥ : SimpleGraph V)) = 1 :=\n  -- mμ(⊥) has exactly one vertex, none\n  one_le_cliqueNum.antisymm'\n    (by simpa using mμ((⊥ : SimpleGraph V)).cliqueNum_le_natCard)\nlemma cliqueNum_mycielskian_bot [Nonempty V] :\n    ω mμ((⊥ : SimpleGraph V)) = 2 := by\n  classical\n  -- none and vʳ are adjacent, giving a 2-clique\n  obtain ⟨v⟩ := ‹Nonempty V›\n  have h2 : mμ(⊥).IsNClique 2 {none, vʳ} :=\n    is2Clique_pair_iff.mpr (by simp)\n  -- moreover mμ(⊥) is 3-clique-free since ⊥ is trivially triangle-free\n  exact h2.cliqueNum_eq_of_cliqueFree (cliqueFree_bot (by norm_num)).mycielskian\nThe last few exercises are on independent sets in complete bipartite graphs (and\ntheir Mycielskians).24. In a complete bipartite graph the non-neighbours of a left vertex are precisely\nthe left part, and similarly for right vertices.lemma completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inl_iff {W : Type*} {v : V} {x : V ⊕ W} :\n    ¬(completeBipartiteGraph V W).Adj (.inl v) x ↔\n      x ∈ Set.range .inl := by\n  cases x <;> simp\nlemma completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inr_iff {W : Type*} {w : W} {x : V ⊕ W} :\n    ¬(completeBipartiteGraph V W).Adj (.inr w) x ↔\n      x ∈ Set.range .inr := by\n  cases x <;> simp\n25. An independent set of a complete bipartite graph is contained entirely in the\nleft part or entirely in the right part: if it contains a left vertex then all its\nvertices are non-neighbours of that vertex, so all lie in the left part.lemma completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_subset_left_or_right {W : Type*}\n    {s : Set (V ⊕ W)} (hs : (completeBipartiteGraph V W).IsIndepSet s) :\n    s ⊆ Set.range .inl ∨ s ⊆ Set.range .inr := by\n  by_cases h : ∃ v, .inl v ∈ s\n  · obtain ⟨v, hv⟩ := h\n    exact Or.inl fun x hx =>\n      completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inl_iff.mp fun hadj => hs hv hx hadj.ne hadj\n  · refine Or.inr fun x hx => ?_\n    rcases x with x | x\n    · exact absurd ⟨x, hx⟩ h\n    · exact Set.mem_range_self x\n26. Any Finset contained in the range of a function f : α → β with finite\ndomain has at most Nat.card α elements. In particular a Finset (V ⊕ W) contained\nin the left part has at most Nat.card V elements (and similarly on the right),\nsince the left part is the range of Sum.inl.lemma _root_.Finset.card_le_of_subset_range {α β : Type*} [Finite α] {f : α → β}\n    {s : Finset β} (h : ↑s ⊆ Set.range f) : #s ≤ Nat.card α := by\n  classical\n  have := Fintype.ofFinite α\n  -- s is contained in the image of univ under f, which has at most #univ\n  -- elements\n  have hsub : s ⊆ univ.image f := fun x hx => by simpa using h hx\n  simpa using (card_le_card hsub).trans card_image_le\n27. Any set of vertices contained in one part of a complete bipartite graph is\nindependent, since a part contains no edges (we only need this for the right part).lemma isIndepSet_completeBipartiteGraph_of_subset_range_inr {W : Type*}\n    {s : Set (V ⊕ W)} (h : s ⊆ Set.range .inr) :\n    (completeBipartiteGraph V W).IsIndepSet s := by\n  intro u hu v hv hne hadj\n  obtain ⟨x, rfl⟩ := h hu\n  obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := h hv\n  simp at hadj\n28. The independence number of a complete bipartite graph is the size of the larger\npart; we state this as ⍺ K[a, b] = b when a ≤ b. The right part is a b-element\nindependent set, and by Exercises 25 and 26 no independent set is larger.lemma indepNum_completeBipartiteGraph {a b : ℕ} (h : a ≤ b) :\n    ⍺ K[a, b] = b := by\n  apply IsNIndepSet.indepNum_eq_of_indepSetFree (s := univ.map .inr)\n  · exact ⟨isIndepSet_completeBipartiteGraph_of_subset_range_inr (by simp), by simp⟩\n  · intro s hs\n    rcases completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_subset_left_or_right hs.isIndepSet\n      with hL | hR\n    · grind [hs.card_eq, card_le_of_subset_range hL, Nat.card_fin]\n    · grind [hs.card_eq, card_le_of_subset_range hR, Nat.card_fin]\nIf we want to bound the independence number of the Mycielskian of a complete\nbipartite graph we need a bound on the size of any independent set s of K[a, b]\nplus the size of the complement of its open neighbourhood N(s)ᶜ. We give this bound\nin the next exercise. The last exercise uses le_indepNum_mycielskian and\nmycielskian_indepSet_card_le from the starred section\nIndependence Number of the Mycielskian.29. Any independent set s of K[a, b] (with a ≤ b) satisfies\n#s + #N(s)ᶜ ≤ 2 * b. We state this with an abstract finset t in place of\nN(s)ᶜ, characterized by its membership property, so that the lemma applies\nwhatever decidability instances N(s)ᶜ is written with. If s is empty then t\nis all a + b vertices; otherwise s lies in a single part (Exercise 25) and\nt lies in the same part (every vertex of the other part is a neighbour of any\nvertex of s), so each has size at most that of the part.lemma completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_card_add_le {a b : ℕ} (hab : a ≤ b)\n    {s t : Finset (Fin a ⊕ Fin b)} (hs : K[a, b].IsIndepSet ↑s)\n    (ht : ∀ x, x ∈ t ↔ ∀ y ∈ s, ¬K[a, b].Adj y x) :\n    #s + #t ≤ 2 * b := by\n  rcases s.eq_empty_or_nonempty with rfl | ⟨x, hx⟩\n  · -- s = ∅ : t has at most a + b ≤ 2 * b vertices\n    grind [Fintype.card_sum, Fintype.card_fin, t.card_le_univ]\n  rcases completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_subset_left_or_right hs with hL | hR\n  · -- s lies in the left part, and so does t\n    obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := hL (mem_coe.mpr hx)\n    have h1 : #s ≤ a := by simpa using card_le_of_subset_range hL\n    have h2 : #t ≤ a := by\n      simpa using card_le_of_subset_range fun z hz =>\n        completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inl_iff.mp ((ht z).mp (mem_coe.mp hz) _ hx)\n    grind\n  · -- s lies in the right part, and so does t\n    obtain ⟨y, rfl⟩ := hR (mem_coe.mpr hx)\n    have h1 : #s ≤ b := by simpa using card_le_of_subset_range hR\n    have h2 : #t ≤ b := by\n      simpa using card_le_of_subset_range fun z hz =>\n        completeBipartiteGraph_not_adj_inr_iff.mp ((ht z).mp (mem_coe.mp hz) _ hx)\n    grind\n30. The independence number of the Mycielskian of K[a, b] is 2 * b whenever\na ≤ b and b ≠ 0: the right part B is an independent set with B ⊆ N(B)ᶜ,\nso it contributes at least 2 * b, and no independent set does better by\nExercise 29. (Note that b ≠ 0 is needed: mμ(K[0, 0]) still has the vertex\nnone, so its independence number is 1.)lemma indepNum_mycielskian_completeBipartiteGraph {a b : ℕ} [NeZero b]\n    (h : a ≤ b) : ⍺ mμ(K[a, b]) = 2 * b := by\n  refine le_antisymm ?_ ?_\n  · -- ≤ : a maximum independent set T of mμ(K[a, b]) satisfies\n    -- #T ≤ #A + #N(A)ᶜ ≤ 2 * b, where A is the left part of T\n    obtain ⟨T, hT⟩ := mμ(K[a, b]).exists_isNIndepSet_indepNum\n    rw [← hT.card_eq]\n    exact le_trans (mycielskian_indepSet_card_le hT.isIndepSet)\n      (completeBipartiteGraph_isIndepSet_card_add_le h\n        (isIndepSet_preimage_left_finset hT.isIndepSet)\n        fun _ => by simp)\n  · -- ≥ : the right part is an independent set s with #s = b and s ⊆ N(s)ᶜ\n    have hind : K[a, b].IsIndepSet ↑(univ.map .inr : Finset (Fin a ⊕ Fin b)) :=\n      isIndepSet_completeBipartiteGraph_of_subset_range_inr (by simp)\n    refine le_trans ?_ (le_indepNum_mycielskian hind)\n    have h1 : #(univ.map .inr : Finset (Fin a ⊕ Fin b)) = b := by simp\n    have h2 := card_le_card hind.subset_compl_openNeighborhoodFinset\n    grind\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Cliques","header":"6.8. Exercises","id":"/Cliques/#section-exercises-cliques"}});